;; `ignore` on a `Result`, which does not help.
;;
;; This is the case that makes the must-use pass know one name. `ignore` answers
;; `void`, so a value handed to it is not in statement position at all — the
;; walk would never look at it, and level three would be defeated by four
;; characters. So the argument of an `ignore` is checked as well.
;;
;; Matched on the trait and the method rather than on the identifier, because a
;; second trait with an `ignore` of its own would otherwise be caught by it.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: `ignore` does not make it allowed
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Handle it with `match`, or take the value out with `unwrap`

(import (std prelude))

(defun (main args)
  (ignore (try (string-length "hi") #:catch (System.Exception)))
  0)
